You are a senior front-end engineer and a Windows 98 obsessive. Build a pixel-accurate, fully interactive Windows 98 desktop environment in the browser for the project in the attached README.md: "SHREE CYBER ZONE — PC 03 (ADMIN)", a cyber café admin machine from Bhilai, 1998.

GOAL
Visually and behaviorally it must be indistinguishable from real Windows 98 SE. All creativity goes into the CONTENT (files, lore, jokes, easter eggs), never into the chrome. If a design choice isn't how Windows 98 did it, don't make it.

HARD CONSTRAINTS
- Vanilla HTML/CSS/JS, no build step, no UI libraries, no frameworks. Static files that deploy to GitHub Pages / Vercel / Netlify.
- No emoji, rounded corners, drop shadows, gradients (except the title-bar gradient noted below), fade transitions, or modern fonts anywhere in the UI.
- No real people's photos or likenesses. All lore is fictional and satirical, exactly as written in the README.
- Original or public-domain assets only. Draw icons as CSS/SVG/pixel-art data URIs and generate sounds with the Web Audio API.
- Build in small steps and give me a runnable result after each one.

VISUAL SPEC (follow exactly)
- Desktop: solid #008080. Icons are 32x32 pixel art, 16-color palette, no anti-aliasing (image-rendering: pixelated), label under the icon in white 11px, navy selection highlight with a dotted focus rectangle.
- Font: "MS Sans Serif", fallback Tahoma, Arial, 11px body, 12px bold for titles. Disable font smoothing where possible.
- Window chrome: 3px outer frame built from the classic 4-layer bevel (#FFFFFF highlight, #DFDFDF, #808080 shadow, #000000 outer shadow). Raised for buttons/windows, sunken for inputs/status bars.
- Title bar: active = gradient #000080 to #1084D0, white bold text, with a 16x16 app icon. Inactive = gradient #808080 to #B5B5B5. Buttons: minimize, maximize/restore, close, 16x14, bevelled, with pixel-drawn glyphs. Double-click the title bar to maximize.
- Controls: buttons (#C0C0C0, black 1px outline, inner highlight, dotted focus ring, sunken on press), checkboxes, radio buttons, dropdowns, tabs, list views with column headers, tree views, status bars, 3D scrollbars with arrow buttons and the dithered track, menu bars with underlined mnemonics, context menus.
- Taskbar: 28px, raised, Start button with the 4-color flag logo, quick-launch icons with a grab handle, window buttons (the active one looks pressed/sunken), a sunken system tray with a live clock. Hovering the clock shows the full date tooltip.
- Start menu: left vertical "Windows 98" banner, the exact item order (Windows Update, Programs, Favorites, Documents, Settings, Find, Help, Run, Log Off, Shut Down) with submenu arrows and flyouts, hover = navy highlight.
- Cursors: custom pixel arrow, hourglass while busy, and text I-beam. Use cursor: url(data:...) with a fallback.
- Dialogs: modal message boxes with the correct icons (information, warning, error, question), default-button focus, and the system "ding" sounds.
- Boot sequence (~4s): black screen with the Windows 98 cloud splash, then the startup sound (synthesized), then the login dialog, then the desktop.

BEHAVIOR SPEC
- Window manager: drag by title bar, resize from all 8 edges/corners, minimize to the taskbar, maximize/restore, close, z-order focus, Alt+Tab, no easing (snap instantly).
- A virtual file system (JSON): drives, folders, files with type icons, sizes, dates, hidden entries, and the ability to type a path like C:\BACKROOM$ into the Explorer address bar.
- Apps (priority order): Explorer, Notepad, Start menu + Run box, Café Manager 98, Session Log, Internet Explorer (fake 1998 pages), Spreadsheet viewer, Minesweeper, Solitaire, Paint, Media Player, Printer Queue, BSOD, Shut Down screens.
- Right-click context menus on the desktop, files, and the taskbar. Drag icons around the desktop. Recycle Bin that "restores" badly.
- Sound: click, error, startup, shutdown, modem handshake, dog bark, ceiling-fan hum. All synthesized with Web Audio.

CREATIVE LAYER (content only)
Implement the story, files, endings and all 15 easter eggs from the attached README. Make the surface feel boring and warm (rates, a dog named Tommy, an AC complaint letter, a cricket match stuck at 98/2) so the discoveries in C:\BACKROOM$ hit hard. Add extra deadpan, dry-humor touches of your own that fit the Indian cyber café of 1998 (Rs. pricing, chai breaks, "please do not touch the monitor" signs, a floppy-disk lending register). Nothing is explained by a narrator; every reveal is a file the user opens.

